{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggwPZwYNLZt6S8CxhTK2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Setup.** I read ν₁ = 10Dq from ⁴A₂→⁴T₂, take B from ⁴A₂→⁴T₁(a), and take C from the spin-forbidden ²E band. I did this by hand. The `ts_fit_spectrum` tool misassigned the bands, so I discarded its result (Dq ≈ 1282, B ≈ 936).\n\n**Dq.** ν₁ = 26 600 cm⁻¹ = 10Dq, so Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹.\n\n**B.** The ⁴T₁(F) and ⁴T₁(P) states mix. The lower root gives ν₂:\n\nν₂ = 15Dq + 7.5B − ½·√(225B² + 100Dq² − 180Dq·B)\n\nI checked this formula on Cr(NH₃)₆³⁺ (Dq = 2150, B = 650). It gives about 28 400 cm⁻¹, close to the observed ⁴T₁ band near 28 500 cm⁻¹.\n\nSetting ν₂ = 32 500 with Dq = 2660:\n\n- The square-root term must equal 14 800 + 15B.\n- Squaring gives 219.04×10⁶ + 444 000B + 225B² = 225B² + 707.56×10⁶ − 478 800B.\n- The B² terms cancel, leaving 922 800B = 488.5×10⁶.\n- So B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹.\n\n**C.** I used the second-order approximation for the ²E state: E(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/(10Dq). I did not check this against the tool.\n\n- 9B = 4765 cm⁻¹.\n- 90B²/(10Dq) = 90(529)²/26 600 ≈ 947 cm⁻¹.\n- So 3C = 12 820 − 4765 + 947 ≈ 9002, which gives C ≈ 3000 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Result.** Dq ≈ 2660 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹, C ≈ 3000 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 5.7). B is about 58% of the free-ion value, which indicates strong covalency. C is only approximate because of the approximation used for ²E.\n\n{\"Dq\": 2660, \"B\": 529, \"C\": 3000, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from ν1=10Dq, B from the ⁴T₁(a) band via the 2x2 T₁ matrix, C from the ²E energy (approximate formula).\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3608,"cache_read_input_tokens":7996,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3608,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnost